    "lawSection": "",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S1980\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\nAn act to amend the general municipal law, in relation to protecting\nhistoric districts and landmarks from substantial deterioration by\nneglect\n\n PURPOSE :\nThe bill clarifies by specifying as part of provisions of law for the\nprotection of historical places, buildings and works of art the\nauthority of municipalities to protect historic buildings from\ndeterioration from improper maintenance or lack of maintenance by the\nowner or person responsible for the building.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\nAmends sections 96-a and 119-dd of the General Municipal law to\nprovide that historic preservation regulations may prohibit the\nimproper maintenance or lack of maintenance of any property in a\ndesignated historic district, or any landmark which results in the\nsubstantial deterioration of such property and threatens its continued\nstability and preservation.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\nThere have been increasing reports from cities like New York City,\nAlbany and Buffalo and from other traditional cities around the nation\nof owners of historic properties failing to properly maintain their\nbuilding resulting in what has been termed \"demolition by neglect\".\nSome cities have enacted regulations to prohibit demolition by neglect\nand authority to do so can be implied. However, to protect\nmunicipalities seeking to prevent demolition by neglect from the\nnuisance of law suits, it is appropriate to clearly specify the\nauthority for this type of regulation.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n2007-08:  Passed Assembly\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\nThis legislation has no direct fiscal impacts.  Its implementation is\nvoluntary on the part of the municipalities.  It may have the effect\nof saving municipalities the expense of legal challenges to their\nauthority that is not specifically clarified.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect on the 90th day after it shall have become\na law.\n",
